Git and GitHub Topics Learned

- Git: A distributed version control system used to track changes in source code.
- GitHub: A cloud platform used to host and manage Git repositories.
- Repository: A storage location for a project's files and version history.
- Local Repository: Repository stored on a local computer.
- Remote Repository: Repository hosted on a server such as GitHub.
- git clone: Creates a local copy of a remote repository.
- git init: Initializes a new Git repository.
- git add: Stages files for the next commit.
- git commit: Saves staged changes with a message.
- git push: Uploads local commits to the remote repository.
- git pull: Downloads and merges changes from the remote repository.
- Branch: An independent line of development.
- git merge: Combines changes from one branch into another.
- git rebase: Reapplies commits on top of another branch to maintain a linear history.
- git cherry-pick: Applies a specific commit from one branch to another.
- Merge Conflict: Occurs when Git cannot automatically merge changes.
- git log: Displays the commit history of a repository.
